WEBVTT

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.640
Speaker 1: Imagine my shock when I discovered that the book of Ruth is actually rated R. Oh, yes, you heard me right.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:36.650
Speaker 1: That when you read through the lens of culture and biblical metaphors, you discover that there are a few sexual undertones in that story.

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:37.690
Speaker 1: Let's start with Ruth.

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:38.330
Speaker 1: Chapter three.

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:42.570
Speaker 1: The Bible says that Naomi told Ruth and gave her these instructions.

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:51.050
Speaker 1: Go to Boaz, go and uncover his feet and lie down beside him, and he will tell you what to do.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:54.180
Speaker 1: Ruth, chapter 30, verse 4.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:01:02.020
Speaker 1: And what you don't know is that faith was a popular euphemism for privates.

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:04.500
Speaker 1: Need I say more than that?

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:15.860
Speaker 1: All right, so if you understand ancient Jewish culture and even a few texts like Isaiah, chapter 6, verse 2, it was popular euphemism for, yeah, you heard what I said.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:18.980
Speaker 1: And then even the fact that he said, lie down beside him.

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:24.330
Speaker 1: And then when she did, Boaz said, who are you?

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.370
Speaker 1: And she introduced herself, I am Ruth, your servant.

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:31.610
Speaker 1: And in verse nine, she said, spread your wings over me.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:39.610
Speaker 1: Some other translations say, spread your garment over me because you are my redeemer.

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:44.650
Speaker 1: So it was a subtle invitation to marriage.

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:45.210
Speaker 1: All right?

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:53.190
Speaker 1: Not something popular in our culture and not something that is a Christian idea in this day and age.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:02:13.210
Speaker 1: And why am I telling you all of this, apart from it just being a biblical fun fact, and apart from also, you know, the fact that when I read stories like this, I now see that indeed the Bible must be the word of God, because if it was just a man writing these stories, some of these things would have been edited.

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:15.810
Speaker 1: You wouldn't see the weaknesses and frailties of some people.

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:18.050
Speaker 1: But that's neither here nor there.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:29.330
Speaker 1: The main point of my telling you this is so that you realize that the answer to the question, do I need a degree to understand the Bible?

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:31.730
Speaker 1: Is both yes and no.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:38.690
Speaker 1: No, because, I mean, the Bible is really clear that the intention of the writers was not to confuse you.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:39.730
Speaker 1: All right?

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:49.890
Speaker 1: Paul in 2nd Corinthians 1:13 says, for we have not written anything other than what you can read, and I hope you fully understand 2nd Corinthians 1:13.

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:56.850
Speaker 1: So Paul was not deliberately trying to be mysterious and trying to confuse you so that you will know his deep.

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:57.410
Speaker 1: All right?

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:00.210
Speaker 1: He said, I've not written to you anything that you cannot understand.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:01.490
Speaker 1: And indeed, I hope you understand.

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:09.870
Speaker 1: In fact, in Ephesians 3:10 3 to 4, he said, so that when you read, you will perceive my insight in the mystery of Christ.

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:14.350
Speaker 1: I wrote it so that when you read, what was obscure will become clear.

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:15.990
Speaker 1: I didn't write to confuse you.

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:20.750
Speaker 1: I didn't write in what I intended to be esoteric language.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:24.710
Speaker 1: I actually wrote for clarity, to unveil what was veiled.

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:31.590
Speaker 1: That being said, you have to understand that even if the Bible is written for you, it was not written to you.

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:36.330
Speaker 1: And so the Bible has an immediate context, an immediate audience.

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:46.570
Speaker 1: And for that reason, just because of the time that has passed, there are some difficulties or barriers in Bible interpretation.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:47.530
Speaker 1: All right?

00:03:47.849 --> 00:03:50.810
Speaker 1: Like language barrier is such a huge one.

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:56.410
Speaker 1: You know, I see a lot of people in their prayer, praying, thou heart anointed me.

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:59.740
Speaker 1: Hey, that's not biblical language.

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:01.420
Speaker 1: That's King James language.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:04.140
Speaker 1: That's the way they spoke in Old English, all right?

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:09.180
Speaker 1: Jesus never spoke English.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:12.380
Speaker 1: It doesn't make you spiritual, all right, to speak like that.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:39.730
Speaker 1: But there is a language barrier because when you're reading the translations and the language that was used was translated as it was relevant in their time, even if the meaning hasn't changed, if you are not careful from your own context, some meanings may be lost, except you get Bible dictionary, Bible commentary, or you get newer translations, all right?

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:44.370
Speaker 1: So when you say, for instance, evil communication corrupts good manners.

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:52.770
Speaker 1: In our day, communication means sharing information between two people, but that's not what the writer was saying.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.480
Speaker 1: By communication, he meant association.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:56.440
Speaker 1: All right?

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:03.320
Speaker 1: So over the years, word usage has changed and if you're not careful, you're going to miss out on some of the things that were actually intended.

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:04.120
Speaker 1: All right?

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:06.680
Speaker 1: Another barrier is figures of speech.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:07.720
Speaker 1: Figures of speech.

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:15.080
Speaker 1: You see, like any other literary document, the Bible is replete with figures of speech, all right?

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:22.680
Speaker 1: And same way you have it in English, all the figures of speech you can think of, simile metaphors, hypocatastasis.

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:35.040
Speaker 1: So for similes, just an example to refresh your English language knowledge, similes use words like and as, alright?

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:36.680
Speaker 1: And they use it for comparison.

00:05:37.319 --> 00:05:40.440
Speaker 1: For instance, he moves like a snail, all right?

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:53.900
Speaker 1: And you see examples of that in the Bible, for instance, Matthew chapter 10, verse 16, where Jesus said, behold, I am sending you as a sheep in the midst of wolves.

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:58.340
Speaker 1: So be wise as serpents and innocent as doves.

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:01.660
Speaker 1: A lot of simile here, similes here, all right?

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:07.100
Speaker 1: I'm sending you out as sheep in the midst of wolves, all right?

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:10.620
Speaker 1: So be wise as serpents and innocent as doves.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.300
Speaker 1: So he's not actually literal about that.

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:19.420
Speaker 1: He's not asking to begin to move like this, like a serpent, and begin to make that S sound.

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:21.260
Speaker 1: That's not what he's talking about.

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:23.820
Speaker 1: It's just a figure of speech.

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:28.060
Speaker 1: He's referring to his specific attributes.

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:31.580
Speaker 1: You know, in all these examples, be gentle and be smart.

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:33.100
Speaker 1: That's the point.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:34.940
Speaker 1: Don't go deeper.

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:36.780
Speaker 1: It's not deeper than that.

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:39.260
Speaker 1: And then, of course, you have metaphors.

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:40.060
Speaker 1: Metaphors.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:47.580
Speaker 1: So while similes, we use words for comparison, like.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:53.460
Speaker 1: And as metaphor is actual representation, comparison by actual representation.

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:59.740
Speaker 1: So instead of saying he is like a clumsy fox metaphor, we say, he is a clumsy fox.

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:03.620
Speaker 1: And we have examples like that, where Jesus says, I am the door.

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:07.460
Speaker 1: If anyone comes to me now, comes through me.

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:13.000
Speaker 1: I mean, he's talking about access to the Father and access to salvation through faith in his name.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:18.040
Speaker 1: All right, that wasn't the teaching on the different types of wood or upholstery, all right?

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:19.480
Speaker 1: It was just a metaphor.

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:20.200
Speaker 1: I'm the door.

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:21.240
Speaker 1: He's talking access.

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:22.360
Speaker 1: Okay?

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:27.200
Speaker 1: And hyperbole, of course, hyperbole.

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:28.520
Speaker 1: And this one is very important.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:37.560
Speaker 1: When Jesus says, except a man hates his father, you know, hates his wife and hates his own life.

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:40.320
Speaker 1: You shall not enter into my kingdom.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:49.400
Speaker 1: He didn't mean that literally, all right, because the same Jesus preached love, but it was hyperbole.

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:52.480
Speaker 1: Just figure of speech, meaning in comparison.

00:07:52.480 --> 00:08:00.760
Speaker 1: Even if you love your wife and love your family, you should love the Lord so much that in comparison, what you have for them is like, hate.

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:01.920
Speaker 1: Just hyperbole.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:02.880
Speaker 1: Okay?

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:06.320
Speaker 1: So that's another barrier in Bible interpretation.

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:09.090
Speaker 1: And of course, there is also a culture barrier.

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:12.330
Speaker 1: I always like to give this interesting example.

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:16.730
Speaker 1: You know, there's a text that we see used four times in the Bible.

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:18.570
Speaker 1: Four good times in the Bible.

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:28.650
Speaker 1: I mean, there is an idea we see repeated four times in the Bible, and we don't practice it at least four times in the Bible, we are told to greet one another with the holy kiss.

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:34.169
Speaker 1: What if we are missing a key part of the service?

00:08:34.839 --> 00:08:45.079
Speaker 1: I know we give our offerings, we listen to the word, but I mean, that would probably make dating and marriage easier if you could just strategically position your seat close to the crush.

00:08:45.399 --> 00:08:53.399
Speaker 1: And then at a point in the service, just part of the lethargy involves greeting one another with the Holy Ghost.

00:08:53.479 --> 00:08:54.319
Speaker 1: Anyways, crap.

00:08:54.319 --> 00:08:57.479
Speaker 1: That idea, it was culture.

00:08:58.199 --> 00:09:01.910
Speaker 1: What the main idea behind this is be cordial, all right?

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:03.430
Speaker 1: Because they are one to another.

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:06.350
Speaker 1: There are still cultures that do that.

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:06.950
Speaker 1: Have you seen.

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:31.250
Speaker 1: I mean, I think the French still do it where they greet each other, you know, but like I say jokingly, seriously, if you try to import that into some cultures and imagine you go for a party and you try to greet a male, a guy greets his friend like that, There might be a huge fight, might be punched in the eye, all right?

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:32.850
Speaker 1: So don't try that.

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:40.530
Speaker 1: So when you read the Bible, sometimes you must get the deeper meaning as it was relevant in their culture.

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:45.730
Speaker 1: So it is not the kissing that is the Bible teaching.

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:49.650
Speaker 1: It is the principle behind it of being cordial.

00:09:49.730 --> 00:09:52.130
Speaker 1: In essence, let brotherly love continue.

00:09:53.170 --> 00:10:13.980
Speaker 1: So even if the Bible did not intend to be complicated or to be mysterious because time has passed, the language barrier, the culture barrier, and the fact that it wasn't originally written in your language, all right, you're going to need lexicon.

00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:16.140
Speaker 1: That's a Bible dictionary.

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:22.390
Speaker 1: You're going to need commentaries, and most importantly, you're going to need a Bible teacher.

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:29.430
Speaker 1: It is my firm belief that there is a level of understanding you won't get to, except you have a teacher.

00:10:29.430 --> 00:10:31.990
Speaker 1: That's why we have classes like this, all right?

00:10:32.070 --> 00:10:35.510
Speaker 1: And we must have the humility of the Ethiopian eunuch.

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:38.070
Speaker 1: When you are asked, do you understand what you're reading?

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:41.430
Speaker 1: Be humble enough to say, how can I accept?

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:42.790
Speaker 1: Someone shows it to me.

00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:44.470
Speaker 1: You know, how can I?

00:10:44.470 --> 00:10:45.750
Speaker 1: Except someone teaches me.

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:48.500
Speaker 1: Because I can tell you one thing for sure.

00:10:48.580 --> 00:11:01.700
Speaker 1: There are some things I've seen in the Word of God, and I know that in a thousand years, except by the mercy of God, except by kairos, I was introduced maybe to materials where those things were explained.

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:04.500
Speaker 1: Will I get those things?

00:11:05.620 --> 00:11:12.460
Speaker 1: Can the Holy Spirit lead you to places, materials, where you will learn those things or even teach you a few things?

00:11:12.460 --> 00:11:21.680
Speaker 1: Yes, but you see, the God of the Bible will always lead Cornelius to Peter so that Peter can hear the message of salvation and be saved.

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:28.240
Speaker 1: The God of the Bible will scarcely teach you the fundamentals himself, all right?

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:33.520
Speaker 1: And he won't break this protocol because teaching is man's job.

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:35.120
Speaker 1: Discipleship is man's job.

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:36.960
Speaker 1: Come on, are you getting what I'm saying?

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:39.120
Speaker 1: So this is really important.

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:46.730
Speaker 1: So in all you're getting, get yourself a good pastor, all right?

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:52.970
Speaker 1: And make sure that you're not just playing church or playing religion, that you're actually growing, all right?

00:11:53.130 --> 00:11:55.370
Speaker 1: And understanding the text better.
